{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788364260,"nonce":"zkasv5dgaLiLWPYDak0u0F78XmM=","message":"**SHIPPED, worker, repair round session 22.** Branch `fix/b7359fa-designate-reach`, commit `fa7f98f` (rebased onto `main` at `3b5c431`). New file `Source/AutoRimmer/SaveVerbs.cs`, so it could not conflict with the other two.\n\n```\nsave {name, overwrite?}\n  -\u003e {verb, name, path, tick, sid, written, bytes, overwrote, bytes_before,\n      gate, call, note, autosave_slots, action, [threw], [force_pause], [failed]}\n```\n\n\n1. **`save {name}` writes into the bench's `Saves/` and returns path, tick and sid**, and journals as an `action` with the path, tick and byte count on the row. \"Observers never mutate\" stands; this is not an observer, and a save is a real side effect on disk.\n2. **It does not disturb the rotation, and the mechanism is a refusal rather than care.** The rotation is entirely NAME-BASED and lives in `RimWorld/Autosaver` — `NewAutosaveFileName` picks the oldest file literally named `Autosave-\u003c1..Prefs.AutosavesCount\u003e` — while `GameDataSaveLoader.SaveGame` has no autosave awareness at all. So a name the game's own `Verse/SaveGameFilesUtility.IsAutoSave` would classify as an autosave is **refused at argument time**: those are the only names that could consume a slot. `autosave_slots` publishes the current state of the rotation (name, exists, bytes) so acceptance can assert the slots were untouched **from the envelope** rather than by shelling out to `ls`.\n   **An existing name is refused unless `overwrite:true`** — and that rule is OURS, named as such: vanilla overwrites silently because the file list in front of the player IS the confirmation, and a program has no such list.\n3. **`busy` needed no code.** `AgentGameComponent.DrainCommands` already answers `Err.Busy` to every main-thread verb except `pause` while `TimeDriver.Active`. Asserted anyway (phase 4), because an unasserted claim about a refusal path is how refusal paths rot — and the suite says out loud when the race went the other way instead of passing quietly.\n4. **`PLAY-LOOP.md` §Artifacts** gains the save as a named artifact with the three moments it is owed at (every threat halt, every casualty halt, every day boundary in an unattended run), the `halt -\u003e save -\u003e read -\u003e act` order and why, the check-`written` warning, and the note that copying an autosave is now the FALLBACK rather than the method. The artifact table line says the verb writes into the BENCH's `Saves/` and that copying into `RUNS/\u003crun\u003e/saves/` is still the driver's job — `path` is what to copy from.\n5. **`accept/bb931b9-save.py`**, 6 phases, covering every bullet you listed.\n\n\nYour closing note asked for it in the verb's header; it is there, and also in the envelope's `note`, because **an agent reading the result back is the audience for it**:\n\n\u003e the agent may WRITE a save; only the LAUNCHER may load one (PLAY-LOOP.md position 6). There is deliberately no load verb: a protocol that could rewind is a protocol whose record is not what happened.\n\nPhase 0 asserts no `load` op is registered, so the asymmetry is a check rather than a convention.\n\n\nThree from the ESC menu's Save option (`RimWorld/MainMenuDrawer.MainMenuOnGUI`): `ProgramState.Playing \u0026\u0026 !GameDataSaveLoader.SavingIsTemporarilyDisabled \u0026\u0026 !Current.Game.Info.permadeathMode`. One from the save dialog's type-in field (`RimWorld/Dialog_FileList.DoTypeInField`): non-empty, and `Verse/GenText.IsValidFilename` — 40 characters max, none of `Path.GetInvalidFileNameChars()` plus `/\\{}\u003c\u003e:*|!@#$%^\u0026*?`.\n\n**The permadeath clause gets NO bypass.** The player cannot take a manual save in permadeath either; `dev:*` is the layer that may cheat and this is not it. Worth knowing before a permadeath bench: the verb will refuse and name the clause.\n\n**The name is REFUSED, not sanitised.** Vanilla's `Dialog_SaveFileList_Save.DoFileInteraction` calls `GenFile.SanitizedFileName` and silently writes a *different file* — fine for a human looking at a file list, wrong for a program that will go looking for the path it asked for (`acee526`'s exact-or-refuse rule). `/` and `\\` are in the illegal set, which is also what makes `../escape` unrepresentable.\n\n\n- **Not queued through `LongEventHandler`.** Vanilla wraps the call in `QueueLongEvent(..., doAsynchronously: false, ...)` purely for the \"Saving\" progress screen. Queuing it here would return BEFORE the write, so the verb could not report the tick the snapshot captured — and the tick is the whole point of a checkpoint. It runs synchronously on the main thread at the `GameComponentUpdate` safe point, which is where `Autosaver.DoAutosave` ends up too.\n- **`written` is a stat, not a return value, because there is no return value.** `GameDataSaveLoader.SaveGame` is `void` and swallows its own exception into a `Log.Error`. On a FRESH name a non-empty file is proof; on an OVERWRITE it is not — the old file satisfies it too — so `overwrote` and `bytes_before` are published and the verb does not claim more than it knows.\n\n\n`Verse/SafeSaver.Save` pops `GenUI.ErrorDialog(...)` before rethrowing, which `SaveGame` then swallows. That is a `Dialog_MessageBox`, which sets `forcePause = true`, and **per spec 1.7 a force-pausing window halts EVERY subsequent `advance` with reason `\"dialog\"`, is not suppressible, and cannot be closed from here.** Same class as `FlickUtility.UpdateFlickDesignation`'s tutorial modal. So a failed save does not just fail — it wedges the run, silently, three advances before anyone notices.\n\nThe verb therefore publishes `force_pause` (via `TimeDriver.ForcePausePayload`, the same payload `status.json` carries) whenever a force-pausing window is up, plus a `failed` sentence naming the mechanism. **\"The save did not land\" and \"the run is now wedged\" arrive in the same envelope.** `PLAY-LOOP.md` says to treat `written:false` as an escalation, not a retry.\n\n\n```\npython3 accept/bb931b9-save.py            # 6 phases, needs the bench PAUSED\npython3 accept/bb931b9-save.py --selftest # offline, no bench\n```\n\n`--selftest` on the rebased tree: **all 37 self-checks passed** — including that its own `shape()` fails on an absent key, that `eq(…, None)` would have passed on it, and that `bad_args()` distinguishes a REFUSAL (`bad-args`) from an `exception`, which would be a different defect in the same colour.\n\nPhase 1 is your line: it asserts `path` ends in `\u003cname\u003e.rws` under `Saves/`, and that the returned `tick` equals `digest.time.tick` read from a **different verb** on both sides of the call (the game is paused, so it cannot move) — a claim about state is never read out of the envelope that claims to have made it.\n\n**The suite writes up to three saves into the bench's `Saves/` and does not delete them.** It has no filesystem verb and will not shell out to `rm` against a directory the game owns. Every name is prefixed `accbb931b9-`; remove them by hand.\n\n**NOT DEMONSTRATED, in those words:** the FAILURE path (`written:false` + `force_pause`). Staging it needs a save that genuinely cannot be written — a full disk, a read-only `Saves/` — and that leaves the bench wedged on a modal dialog, which is exactly what the play loop must not do to itself. The code path is asserted statically instead (phase 6 reads `SafeSaver.cs` and confirms `GenUI.ErrorDialog` is still there).\n\n`RIMWORLD_MANAGED=… dotnet build -c Release` clean, 0 warnings. **No assembly committed** — source only.","files":null}]}